WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.020
"Book Trailer for How Not to Age"

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:10.740
There may be no such thing
as dying from old age.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:15.000
From a study of more than 42,000
consecutive autopsies,

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.550
centenarians—those who live
past one hundred—

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.440
were found to have succumbed
to diseases

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.500
in 100 percent
of the cases examined.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.980
Though most were perceived,
even by their physicians,

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.960
to have been healthy
just prior to death,

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.620
not one “died of old age.”

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:35.700
They died from disease,
most commonly heart attack.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.133
If aging kills via diseases,
why wasn’t my book

00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:43.320
How Not to Die all the longevity
book anyone needs?

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:46.080
In it, I ran through
what we can do to prevent, arrest,

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.540
or reverse each of our 15
leading causes of death,

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:52.560
starting with heart disease, not only
the #1 killer of centenarians,

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.580
but of men and women in general,
and projected to remain that way

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.802
in the decades to come.
But is it really?

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.360
Because the single greatest
risk factor

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:06.420
for most of our killer diseases
is old age,

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:09.600
one could argue that
the leading cause of death

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.220
is actually aging.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.920
The rate of death
increases exponentially

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.360
for age-related diseases

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.120
such as heart disease, cancer,
stroke, dementia.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:22.440
So yes, in the same age bracket,
having a high cholesterol

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:23.820
can increase
your risk of heart disease

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.080
as much as twentyfold,

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:28.680
but an 80-year-old
may have 500 times the risk

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:32.044
of having a heart attack compared
to someone in their twenties.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:36.060
Yes, eating a plant-based diet
may reduce the risk of dementia

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:37.140
as much as threefold,

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.120
but the difference
in dementia rates

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.560
between those older than 85

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:45.480
compared to younger
than 65 is 300-fold.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:48.120
The reason we focus
on things like cholesterol

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.360
is because it’s a modifiable
risk factor.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.058
But what if the rate of aging
was modifiable too?

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.620
Instead of our current
piecemeal approach

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:01.380
of focusing on individual
degenerative diseases,

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.320
what about slowing down
the aging process itself?

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.140
I can remember as a nerdy kid

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.600
I wanted to cure cancer
when I grew up.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.420
But even if all forms
of cancer were eliminated,

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.760
the average life expectancy
in the US

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.520
would only go up
about three years. Why?

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.340
Because dodging cancer
would just mean delaying death

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.380
from something like
a heart attack or stroke.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:26.160
If one age-related ailment
doesn’t get us, another will.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.560
Rather than playing
“whack-a-mole”

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.600
by tackling each
disease separately,

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.560
progress in decelerating
aging could address

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:36.420
all these issues simultaneously.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.100
Imagine if there
was an intervention

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.620
that didn’t just reduce your risk
of the leading killers,

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:44.040
but also arthritis, osteoporosis,
sensory impairments.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.680
Even just slowing aging seven years,

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:49.560
such that the average
65-year-old, for example,

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.420
would have the health profile
and disease risk

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:54.960
of today’s 58-year-old,
would be expected to cut

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:58.744
everyone’s risk of death, frailty,
and disability in half

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:02.722
(because such risks tend
to double every seven years).

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:05.700
That’s why I wrote How Not to Age.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:08.340
The problem is
the anti-aging field

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:11.160
is said to be a “fertile ground
for cons, scams,

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:12.660
and get-rich-quick schemes.”

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:15.840
As a former president of the
Gerontological Society of America

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:19.140
has written, there have been
“few subjects which have been

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:20.940
more misleading
to the uncritical

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.084
and more proﬁtable
to the unscrupulous.”

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.180
Not only does the popular
literature on the subject harbor

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:29.640
a “huge amount of misinformation,”

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:33.420
but most anti-aging scientists
widely known to the public

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:37.440
are said to be “unscrupulous
purveyors of useless nostrums,”

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:40.886
according to the editor-in-chief
of a leading gerontology journal.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:44.460
It's easy to be swayed
by charismatic gurus,

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.980
but when it comes to something
as life-and-death important

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:49.020
as the health and well-being
of ourselves and our families,

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:52.860
we should rely not on anecdote,
but on evidence.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:55.620
That’s why I cite everything
to the teeth.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.920
How Not to Die had about
2,000 citations;

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:00.660
How Not to Diet, 5,000;

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:04.800
How Not to Age ended up
with 13,000 citations,

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.600
all of which I have hyperlinked

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:10.000
for you online to access
the original sources.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:13.070
My inspiration for writing
How Not to Age

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:14.549
was a consensus document

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:17.640
entitled "Interventions
to Slow Aging in Humans"

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:21.060
compiled by the top researchers
in anti-aging medicine

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:25.380
(the likes of Drs. Longo, Sinclair,
Fontana, a veritable Who’s Who).

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:29.460
They were brought together to identify
the most promising strategies

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:30.540
to combat aging.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.734
They identified a list of
“essential pathways,”

00:04:33.734 --> 00:04:36.253
for example, drugs that can block
the hormone IGF-1,

00:04:36.253 --> 00:04:38.400
or drugs to block
the enzyme TOR.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:40.680
As I looked through
the list, I realized:

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:45.840
Wait, every single one of these pathways
could be regulated through diet.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:50.366
That became the opening
section of this book.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.740
The science of aging
has been called

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:55.800
“the most dynamic and provocative
in modern biology.”

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:58.980
An attempt to classify
the theories of aging published

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:02.580
more than 30 years ago identified
more than 300 such theories,

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:05.220
and the number
has only grown since.

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:09.480
In Part I, I identify the 11
most promising pathways

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:13.140
for slowing the sands of time,
ending each with practical proposals

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:15.600
for targeting them each
naturally with suggestions

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.100
for diet and lifestyle changes.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:21.180
Part II targets the optimal
anti-aging regimen.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:24.480
The odds of living
to age 100 have risen

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:28.020
from approximately 1 in 20 million
to as high as 1 in 50.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:30.000
Why do some make it
and others not?

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.360
It’s not just a matter
of picking better parents.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:36.120
Studies following identical twins
suggest that only

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:38.340
about 25 percent
of the variation in lifespan

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:40.260
is explained by gene inheritance.

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:43.740
The media loves stories
about hard-living centenarians

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:45.960
who attribute their longevity
to some combination

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:49.140
of lard, vodka, and their favorite
brand of cigarette.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.738
But how do centenarians
and supercentenarians

00:05:51.738 --> 00:05:54.540
 (those over 110)
really eat and live?

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:57.120
What are the best and worst
foods and beverages?

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:00.060
What about red wine, and
fish, olive oil, coffee?

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:03.180
I cover the "longevity vitamin"
ergothioneine,

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:05.100
the vegetarian’s Achilles’ heel,

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:08.400
and the ideal exercise
and sleep routines.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:12.480
Then in Part III, I get into
the nitty gritty.

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:16.620
What can we do to preserve our bones,
bowels, bladder, and circulation;

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:18.480
your hair, hearing,
and hormone balance;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:20.460
your immune function
and joint health;

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:23.100
your mind, your muscles;
your sex life and skin;

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:26.520
your teeth, your vision, and finally,
your dignity in death?

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:28.260
There are chapters on each.

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:31.680
Did you know that 85 percent
of bone fracture risk

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:34.140
has nothing to do with
your bone mineral density?

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:36.600
I address that in the
“Preserving Your Bones” chapter,

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:39.180
talking about the pros and cons
of the new osteoporosis drugs,

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:40.620
what supplements to consider,

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:43.740
as well as more natural approaches
to protect your skeleton.

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:46.380
I discuss preserving
your bowel function,

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:48.360
fittingly, in chapter number two.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:51.360
I discuss solutions
to the constipation epidemic,

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:53.640
proper positioning,
laxatives, fiber supplements.

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:56.700
I also compare the six colon cancer
screening strategies

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:58.620
and ways to help prevent it
in the first place,

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:02.160
as well as bladder function—
urinary incontinence,

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:03.600
and prostate enlargement.

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:07.560
In the circulation chapter, I discuss
how having a "normal" cholesterol

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:10.920
or blood pressure is not
so good in a society

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:13.320
where it’s normal
for people to drop dead

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:14.520
of heart attacks and strokes.

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:17.460
I cover statins and stents
and, most importantly,

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:20.760
treating the underlying cause
of artery function failure.

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:24.060
“Preserving Your Hair” refers
to both color and volume.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.720
Why do we gray and lose our hair,
and what we can do about it?

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:29.880
I also cover drugs,
supplements, and surgeries,

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:31.980
as well as dietary
and topical treatments.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:33.962
In “Preserving Your Hearing”

00:07:33.962 --> 00:07:37.295
I break down hearing aids,
earwax removal,

00:07:37.295 --> 00:07:40.200
and the fact that, in some cultures,
there’s no such thing

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:42.120
as age-related hearing loss.

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:45.600
What can we learn from them
to protect our own inner ears?

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:48.251
In the hormone chapter,
I investigate blockbuster

00:07:48.251 --> 00:07:51.960
anti-aging “treatments”
like growth hormone injections,

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:55.140
DHEA, the preservation
of female fertility,

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:58.500
menopausal hormone therapy,
natural alternatives for symptoms

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:02.160
like hot flashes, and the pros
and cons of testosterone therapy

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:03.840
for so-called male “andropause.”

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:07.680
In the immunity chapter,
I cover immune-boosting foods

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:10.320
to improve cancer surveillance
and resistance to infection,

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:13.380
examine the role of supplements
like ginseng, vitamin D, and zinc,

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:15.720
and help you decide
whether to get vaccinated

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:18.180
against the flu,
pneumonia, and shingles.

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:21.108
The joint chapter
touches on back pain,

00:08:21.108 --> 00:08:23.454
but focuses on osteoarthritis,

00:08:23.454 --> 00:08:26.520
covering prescription and
over-the-counter drugs, injections,

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:30.000
knee surgery, topical treatments,
inflammation-dousing foods,

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.820
and supplements, such as
glucosamine and chondroitin.

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:36.780
“Preserving Your Mind”
takes on the dementia epidemic.

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:39.180
I cover Alzheimer’s disease,
vascular dementia,

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:41.640
the rapidly reversible
causes of dementia,

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:43.380
the controversial new drugs,

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:45.480
so-called "brain supplements"
like ginkgo,

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:48.180
the role of aluminum
and chronic infections like HSV,

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:51.180
and, most importantly,
what we can do to prevent, arrest,

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:53.640
and even rewind
cognitive decline.

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:56.400
In “Preserving Your Muscles,”
I tackle what we can do

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:58.860
to combat age-related
muscle loss and frailty,

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:00.896
addressing protein
needs of older adults

00:09:00.896 --> 00:09:02.185
and supplements like creatine,

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:05.201
as well as touch on
essential tremor.

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:08.760
Then, it’s on to
“Preserving Your Sex Life.”

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:11.460
I discuss the best ways,
natural and otherwise,

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:14.040
to improve male and female
sexual interest and function.

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:17.700
I talk about aging body odor,
the best vaginal moisturizers

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:19.440
and lubricants,
and the role of herbs,

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:22.050
such as ashwagandha
and maca root.

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:26.298
(I’ll understand if you want
to skip directly to this chapter.)

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:28.852
Then, it’s all about skincare.

00:09:28.852 --> 00:09:31.179
I cover the pros and cons
of chemical peels,

00:09:31.179 --> 00:09:34.620
laser resurfacing,face-lifts,
Botox, and filler injections;

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:37.390
how we can prevent
and treat wrinkles both topically

00:09:37.390 --> 00:09:39.720
and from the inside
out by what we eat.

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:41.760
What’s the safest sunscreen?

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:43.309
What’s the value
of facial moisturizers,

00:09:43.309 --> 00:09:46.516
alpha hydroxy acids, retinoids,
collagen supplements,

00:09:46.516 --> 00:09:49.080
and anti-aging skin creams
and serums?

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:51.780
I also cover varicose veins
and nail health,

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:54.420
from brittle fingernails
to fungal toenails,

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:57.420
and the role of nail supplements
like biotin.

00:09:57.420 --> 00:09:59.640
In “Preserving Your Teeth,”
I take a bite out

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:01.440
of periodontal disease
and tooth loss,

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:05.040
cover oil pulling and fluoride,
and detail the best mouthwash.

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:09.000
In the vision chapter, I focus
on dietary approaches to cataracts,

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:11.820
glaucoma, and macular degeneration,
including the role

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:13.568
of lutein and zeaxanthin.

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:17.100
And the final chapter in the section,
“Preserving Your Dignity,”

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:20.000
offers the assurance&nbsp;
that you can have control

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:22.714
over your ultimate fate.

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:25.680
The final section,
“Dr. Greger's Anti-Aging Eight,”

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:28.560
is intended to be an actionable
checklist to complement

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:30.540
my Daily Dozen
from How Not to Die.

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:32.392
In addition to the wealth
of recommendations

00:10:32.392 --> 00:10:34.418
throughout the book,
this section is designed

00:10:34.418 --> 00:10:36.420
 to highlight specific foods,
supplements,

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:38.220
or behaviors that have
the potential to offer

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:42.643
the best opportunities to slow
aging or improve longevity.

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:46.140
My aim with this book was to cover
every possible angle

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:49.260
for developing the optimal diet
and lifestyle for the longest,

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:54.163
healthiest lifespan based on the best
available balance of evidence.

